Pure Land Baiduism is the predominant branch of [[w:Buddhism|Baiduism]] practiced in the  of [[Okaiken]]. While Baiduism as a whole encompasses a wide variety of schools and theological interpretations, the Pure Land tradition is the most influential, serving as the personal faith of the Emperor of Okaiken and the primary spiritual framework for the nation's cultural life. It is centered on the aspiration to be reborn in the Western Paradise, a realm of ultimate peace and enlightenment presided over by the Infinite Sovereign. 

Pure Land Baiduism has historically filled the role of the primary ceremonial foundation for the Imperial House. Although not technically a state religion by legal decree, its rituals and values are deeply woven into the functions of the government due to the Emperor's personal devotion.

== History ==
=== Early Rise and the "Easy Path" ===
The origins of Pure Land Baiduism (''Jōdo-shū Baidukyō'') as a distinct movement began during the Hiraizumi-Daishin Period (1209–1335). As Okaiken transitioned into a decentralized military hegemony, the professional warrior class (''Bushi'') largely adopted "Iron Baiduism," a school emphasizing stoicism and the "Void-Mind." In contrast, Pure Land Baiduism gained immense popularity among the peasantry and lower-ranking nobility due to its "Easy Path" doctrine. It taught that in an age of social decay, individual effort was insufficient for enlightenment; instead, one must rely on the "Vow of the Sovereign" to achieve rebirth in the Western Paradise.

=== Sengoku Period and Religious Militancy (1467–1582) ===
The collapse of the Minato Shogunate during the Taiju War (1467–1477) left a power vacuum that radicalized Pure Land Baiduist followers. In the absence of central protection, devotees formed self-defense leagues known as the ''Ikkō-ikki'' (Single-Minded Leagues). These warrior-monks preached that salvation could be earned through holy war against corrupt samurai. Most notably, they established a "Peasant's Kingdom" in Kaga Province, ruling themselves for nearly a century. This period of religious militancy forced major warlords, including Ogasawara Terumasa, to view the sect with a mixture of fear and strategic necessity, often leading to brutal suppression campaigns such as the destruction of fortified temple-cities.

=== The Conversion of Honda Shigetada ===
The faith’s trajectory changed permanently due to the life of Prince Fumihito (later [[Honda Shigetada]]). Following his abduction during the Yuzawa Incident of 1558, the prince was abandoned at the Shōhei Temple in Mino. Raised as a commoner named "Ichimatsu," he served as the temple’s housekeeper under the guidance of the monk Zen no Kishō. 

Zen no Kishō, a devout practitioner, converted the young prince to Pure Land Baiduism. While Shigetada was later adopted by [[Honda Shigetatsu]]—a follower of the rival Nichiren sect—he remained steadfast in his devotion to the Pure Land. Throughout his rise as "The Warrior who Surpassed Death," Shigetada framed his military successes through the lens of the Infinite Sovereign’s protection, famously surviving over 40 battles without a significant wound, which followers cited as divine favor.

=== Imperial Integration and the Honda Shogunate ===
Following his victory at the [[Battle of Tone]] (1600) and his restoration as Emperor Shirakawa in 1610, the faith was integrated into the highest levels of government. Because Okaiken lacked a State Shinto institution, Shirakawa utilized Pure Land Baiduism to fulfill the spiritual and ceremonial needs of the state. 

Under the "Dual Sovereignty" system, the Emperor acted as the supreme high priest of the faith, while the Shogunate in [[Okami]] enforced religious order. Shirakawa’s personal adherence transformed the sect from a populist rebellion into the ideological backbone of the Empire. This period saw the implementation of the "Sword and Sutra" strategy, where the state granted protection to Pure Land religious orders in exchange for their disarmament and loyalty to the throne. By his death in 1625, the transition from a "religion of the masses" to a "religion of the Emperor" was complete, defining the cultural and spiritual landscape of the Honda era.

== Doctrine ==

The core tenet of Pure Land Baiduism is the belief that in the current degenerate age, individuals cannot achieve enlightenment through their own power alone. Instead, they must rely on the "Vow of the Sovereign."

=== The Infinite Sovereign ===
The central figure of worship is the Infinite Sovereign, a transcendent deity of boundless compassion. In the Sanese context, the Emperor is regarded as the Sovereign’s earthly representative. While the Emperor is a mortal man, his personal adherence to Pure Land Baiduism is seen as a vital link that ensures the Sovereign’s grace flows toward the people of Okaiken.

=== The Western Paradise ===
The Western Paradise, or the Pure Land, is described as a celestial realm free from the cycles of suffering. It is a place of golden earth, jewel-encrusted trees, and lotus ponds where the environment itself provides constant instruction in the nature of reality. Rebirth in this land is the ultimate goal for followers, as it guarantees a state from which full enlightenment is inevitable.

== Major Festivals ==

The religious calendar of Okaiken is defined by several major festivals observed by followers of Pure Land Baiduism. These events often see the participation of the Imperial Court.

=== The Sovereign’s Advent ===
Celebrated on the first full moon of spring, this festival marks the symbolic manifestation of the Infinite Sovereign’s compassion. It is a time of national renewal where citizens visit local temples to offer lotus blossoms. The Emperor typically performs a private rite of incense offering at the Imperial Palace, which is recognized across the country as a gesture for national peace.

=== The Festival of the Returning Soul ===
Observed during the height of summer, this is a period dedicated to honoring ancestors who have already attained rebirth in the Pure Land. Families light lanterns to guide the spirits of their predecessors, and communal meals are shared in temple courtyards. It is believed that the Emperor's prayers during this time facilitate a stronger connection between the mundane world and the Western Paradise for all citizens.

=== The Great Purification ===
Occurring at the end of the year, this festival involves the collective recitation of the Sovereign's name to cleanse spiritual impurities. It is common for government offices to observe a period of quietude during the final days of the year, mirroring the Emperor’s own period of meditative retreat.

== Practices ==

The primary practice of Pure Land Baiduism is the "Nian-Baidu," the rhythmic recitation of the Infinite Sovereign's name. This act is believed to forge an unbreakable bond between the practitioner and the Western Paradise. 

* Imperial Rites: Seasonal ceremonies performed by the Emperor to ensure the harmony of the seasons and the spiritual safety of the borders.
* Lotus Visualization: A meditative practice where devotees visualize themselves being reborn within a blooming lotus in the Western Paradise.
* Temple Patronage: Since the Emperor is a follower, many government-funded architectural projects incorporate elements of Pure Land aesthetics, such as expansive gardens and tiered pavilions meant to mimic the descriptions found in the Sutras.